# MTK connsys / WLAN bring-up for the Redmi 10 (MT6768, selene).
#
# The dieD 4.19 kernel has the whole MTK WLAN stack built in
# (CONFIG_WLAN_DRV_BUILD_IN=y, CONFIG_MTK_COMBO_WIFI=y), but the stock 4.14
# kernel does not, which is why WLAN was disabled when this port started. The
# kernel side needs nothing from us: the platform device 18000000.wifi is
# already in the DTB and the driver binds the moment connsys is powered.
#
# What is missing at boot is the userspace power sequence. Nothing touches
# connsys in recovery, so /dev/wmtWifi and wlan0 do not exist:
#
#   wmt_loader    powers the connectivity SoC, creates /dev/stpwmt
#   wmt_launcher  downloads the ROM patch, runs calibration, keeps STP alive
#   /dev/wmtWifi  writing 1 makes the WLAN driver register wlan0
#
# The loaders and the firmware they read are staged in the ramdisk under
# /vendor, so this works before (and without) mounting the stock vendor.
#
# The slow part runs once at boot in a background oneshot service. That keeps
# it off init's critical path while still letting a tap on "Start WLAN" finish
# inside wlan.cpp's 20 s window: by then only `ifconfig wlan0 up` and the
# supplicant start are left.

on boot
    start aera-wlan-boot

# One owner for the power sequence: aera-wlan-up.sh --boot runs wmt_loader,
# starts wmt_launcher, waits for its "Calibration end", then powers the WLAN
# function on. Do NOT also declare wmt_loader / wmt_launcher as separate
# services here: that ran the launcher twice, and two daemons fighting over
# /dev/stpwmt corrupt the STP stream the patch download uses.

# Waits for the launcher to finish the patch download and calibration, then
# powers on the WLAN function and waits for wlan0. All of it is best effort:
# if it fails, the on-demand path below tries again.
service aera-wlan-boot /system/bin/sh /system/bin/aera-wlan-up.sh --boot
    user root
    group root wifi inet net_admin system
    oneshot
    disabled
    seclabel u:r:recovery:s0

# Fired by Wlan::StartInitSupplicantService() (wlan.cpp). AERA sets the
# property and resets it here, so the next tap is a fresh 0->1 transition.
on property:sys.aera.wlan.up=1
    setprop sys.aera.wlan.up 0
    mkdir /tmp/recovery 0777 root root
    mkdir /tmp/recovery/sockets 0777 root root
    start aera-wlan-up

service aera-wlan-up /system/bin/sh /system/bin/aera-wlan-up.sh
    user root
    group root wifi inet net_admin system
    oneshot
    disabled
    seclabel u:r:recovery:s0

# AERA builds its own wpa_supplicant into /system/bin (the MTK vendor one at
# /vendor/bin/hw does not exist in this ramdisk). wlan.cpp waits up to 60 s for
# /tmp/recovery/sockets/wlan0 and a PING/PONG, which is exactly where
# ctrl_interface=wlan0 in /vendor/etc/wifi/wpa_supplicant.conf puts the socket
# once -O points at that directory.
service wpa_supplicant /system/bin/wpa_supplicant -Dnl80211 -iwlan0 -dd -O/tmp/recovery/sockets -c/vendor/etc/wifi/wpa_supplicant.conf
    user root
    group root wifi inet net_admin
    disabled
    seclabel u:r:recovery:s0
